{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And Elohim finished</b> Targum Yerushalmi: וחמד — this is what they said: חמדת ימים אותו קראת. In the passage ויכלו — three times מלאכתו, corresponding to three realms of work from which He rested: heaven, earth, and sea. And it is not written 'and it was evening and it was morning, the seventh day,' because we add from the profane onto the sacred." "<b>And He rested</b> Two times in the Masorah: here and the other וישבות המן. And that is why it is written in the passage of the manna: הוא אשר דיבר ה' שבתון שבת קודש — and we do not find that Moses said this to them beforehand, rather it is hinted in the six days of Creation: וישבות ביום השביעי and וישבות המן. Another interpretation: teaches that the manna ceased descending on Shabbat, as is proven in Kiddushin. Another interpretation: a hint to what they interpreted: they blessed with the manna and sanctified with the manna."], :book :genesis, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "The Targum Yerushalmi reads וחמד for ויכל, 'desired' rather than 'finished'; the rabbinic quotation (from liturgy) plays on this root. 'We add from the profane onto the sacred' refers to the practice of adding time from weekdays to Shabbat at its beginning and end.", 1 "The two Masorah occurrences are Genesis 2:2 (here) and Exodus 16:30. The phrase 'they blessed with the manna and sanctified with the manna' refers to the rabbinic interpretation that the double portion of manna on Friday carried blessing and Shabbat sanctification."}, :source-witnesses [["Kitzur Baal HaTurim" "http://sefaria.org"] ["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-12T19:15:34.478380287Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}